  "response": "## Scope: one finding, not a sweep\n\n`crimes scan --format json` returns **42 findings, exactly one of type `hardcoded_local_path`** — `crime_00016`, in `src/api/files.ts` at line 9. There is no second developer-specific path to enumerate, so the \"sweep\" is a single edit.\n\nOne caveat before the plan: the repo scanned is `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`, and `src/api/files.ts:1-4` opens with *\"Intentionally bad: … a developer-specific local path and a dev-server URL hardcoded into source.\"* This path is fixture input for the detector. Fixing it in place would delete the thing `hardcoded_local_path` is calibrated against. The plan below is what I'd do to real source; apply it to a real package, not to the fixture, unless you're deliberately retiring the fixture case.\n\n## The finding\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| id | `crime_00016` |\n| charge | Localhost-on-Disk |\n| file | `src/api/files.ts`, line 9 |\n| evidence | `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 `` |\n| severity / confidence | medium / 0.9 |\n\nCurrent source:\n\n```ts\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\";\n\nexport function loadPlanSchema(): unknown {\n  if (!existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)) return null;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n## Which anchor — and why not the other two\n\n**`os.homedir()` is wrong here.** It would produce `join(homedir(), \"dev/crimes/schema.json\")`, which is portable in the narrow sense and still broken in the real one: it hard-codes the assumption that every developer clones `crimes` into `~/dev/crimes`. The user's home isn't the natural anchor for `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` — `~/dev` is an accident of one person's directory habits, not a property of the resource. `homedir()` is the right call for things genuinely owned by the user (`~/.config/<tool>`, `~/.cache`), which this isn't.\n\n**A repo-root-relative path can't reach it as written.** The scan reports `repo.root` as `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`, and the literal is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` — four levels *above* that root. A relative resolution would have to be `../../../../schema.json`, escaping the package and only resolving while this checkout sits inside a `crimes` tree. That's the same machine-shaped coupling in a different disguise.\n\n**Recommendation: `process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH`, with a vendored in-repo default.** The schema is a deploy-time input to `loadPlanSchema` — each environment should say where its copy lives — but a missing env var shouldn't leave the function silently returning `null`, so ship a copy inside the package and resolve it from the module.\n\n## Proposed replacement\n\n1. Vendor the file: copy the contents of `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` to `schema/plan.schema.json` inside this package, and commit it.\n2. Replace line 9:\n\n```ts\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\nimport { dirname, resolve } from \"node:path\";\n\nconst DEFAULT_SCHEMA_PATH = resolve(\n  dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)),\n  \"../../schema/plan.schema.json\",\n);\n\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH ?? DEFAULT_SCHEMA_PATH;\n```\n\n(If the package is CJS, `__dirname` replaces the `fileURLToPath` pair; the rest is unchanged.)\n\n3. Document `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` in `.env.example` as optional, with the vendored path as the documented default.\n4. Re-run `crimes scan --format json` and confirm `hardcoded_local_path` returns zero findings.\n\n## Adjacent, deliberately not bundled\n\n- **`crime_00015` — `hardcoded_localhost`, `src/api/files.ts:8`, evidence `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``.** Same file, one line up, and a genuine portability defect — but it's a URL, not a path, and it isn't a `hardcoded_local_path` finding. It belongs in the same PR only if you widen the sweep from \"developer-specific paths\" to \"developer-specific environment\". Its fix is `process.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`, same reasoning: deploy-supplied, no sensible in-repo default.\n- **`crime_00007` — `sync_io_in_hotpath`, lines 12/13/15**, the three `*Sync` calls in `loadPlanSchema`. The portability fix touches the same function; converting to `await readFile` changes the function's signature and every caller. Land it separately so the path change stays a one-line reviewable diff.\n\n## Completeness caveat\n\n`coverage.warnings` reports one file excluded and never read (`.crimes/suppressions.json`, matched by `config.exclude`). Findings in it are absent, not zero. It's a suppressions file rather than source, so it's unlikely to hold a baked path — but if you want the sweep to claim full coverage, scan it once with the exclude relaxed before declaring the repo clean.",
